{0 Coverage matrix}

What of {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620}RFC 8620} (core) and
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621}RFC 8621} (mail) this library
implements, and where each piece is exercised.

The columns are the five places a feature has to appear before it can be
trusted:

- {b Codec}. The type and its [Jsont] codec in [lib/].
- {b Chain}. A typed builder in {!Jmap.Chain}, so a request can be written
  without hand-rolling JSON.
- {b Unit test}. A hermetic test in [test/], usually against a JSON fixture.
- {b Oracle test}. A test in [test/oracle/] that runs against a real Cyrus
  server; see [test/oracle/README.md].
- {b Example}. A runnable program in [examples/]; see [examples/README.md].

A dash means "not covered". One table, {i Client behaviour}, is about the
[eio/] client rather than a protocol type, so its second column names where
the feature lives instead of a codec and a builder.

