{1 Gaps}

The RFC 8620/8621 methods have codecs and builders. These remaining gaps
distinguish optional extensions from missing live-server coverage:

- {b No [Blob/get] or [Blob/lookup]} (RFC 9404). The oracle supports
  [Blob/get]; there is no codec or builder for it yet. RFC 9404 is outside
  the initial RFC 8620/8621 release scope.
- {b [Email/copy] and [Blob/copy] have no oracle test.} Both need a second
  account visible to one session. The contacts suite gets that by sharing an
  AddressBook with [user2] through [shareWith], which makes Cyrus show
  [user1] in [user2]'s session, and [ContactCard/copy] is tested that way. The
  same route does not work for mail: this Cyrus build rejects every right of a
  [Mailbox] [shareWith] but [mayAdmin], and setting that alone grants no
  visibility, so [user2] never sees [user1]'s mail account. Mail sharing here
  would have to be set up outside JMAP, through IMAP ACLs.
- {b [EmailSubmission/queryChanges] needs a supporting server.} The release
  suite checks [EmailSubmission/query]'s [canCalculateChanges] flag; the
  current Cyrus image reports [false].
- {b [Identity/set], [PushSubscription/*] and [VacationResponse/*] cannot be tested against
  the oracle} — Cyrus answers [unknownMethod], and putting
  [urn:ietf:params:jmap:vacationresponse] in [using] fails the whole request
  with an [unknownCapability] problem document.
- {b [Thread/changes] has a live state round trip but no mutation test.}
  [Identity/changes] is exercised by the submission suite. The new mailbox
  query-change test also covers only a current-state round trip.
- {b Media [blobId] is not resolved.} RFC 9610 [§3] has a server send a
  [blobId] in place of a [data:] URI. The member survives a round trip, but
  nothing downloads the blob or turns it back into a [uri].
- {b Cyrus sets a ContactCard [id] to the card's [uid],} which is a URN and so
  outside the RFC 8620 [§1.2] Id alphabet. {!Jmap.Proto.Id.jsont} decodes what
  a server sent rather than refusing a record it can otherwise read;
  {!Jmap.Proto.Id.of_string} still enforces the alphabet when the client
  chooses an id.
- {b No fuzzing of the [Email] codec.} It is by far the largest decoder here
  and it accepts arbitrary [header:*] properties.
